  "id": "DrugBank_DB01164 - MONDO_745",
  "verbalization": "Calcium Chloride is associated with Cardiac Arrest through a chain of indications that involve calcium-containing compounds. The evidence suggests that Calcium Chloride leads to Nephrocalcinosis, a condition characterized by the deposition of calcium salts in the kidneys. This condition then progresses to other calcium-based compounds, such as Calcium Acetate and Calcium Glycerophosphate, which are also linked to Cardiac Arrest. The shared mechanism of calcium deposition and its subsequent impact on cardiac function provide a plausible pathway for the observed relationship. The consistency in the scores across the three paths reinforces the strength of this connection, highlighting a recurring role of calcium in mediating the risk of cardiac arrest. The integration of these pathways underscores the importance of calcium homeostasis in cardiovascular health, suggesting that disruptions in calcium metabolism may contribute to the development of Cardiac Arrest.",
